Furthermore, when I returned my keys on XX/XX/XXXX, I specifically requested a final walk-through, which the on-site staff refused to conduct. This prevented me from seeing or addressing any items they later charged me for and is inconsistent with a fair and transparent inspection process. 

I also have documented emails from XX/XX/XXXX, shortly after move-in, showing that I reported multiple pre-existing defects in the unit ( peeling doors, paint issues, construction imperfections ). These issues existed long before move-out and were acknowledged by management. Despite this, the move-out charges include items that relate to these pre-existing problems, which is improper and unlawful under Texas landlord-tenant law. 

Given these issues, the alleged debt is not valid, was not properly validated, and should not have been sent to collections. 

I am requesting CFPB intervention to : Require XXXX XXXX to cease all collection activity, Ensure the alleged debt is not reported to any credit bureau, Require that the alleged debt be removed entirely, Require XXXX XXXX to remove all improper charges, Require XXXX XXXX to refund my {$360.00} rent overpayment and refund my {$400.00} security deposit that was wrongfully withheld. 

I can provide copies of the move-out inspection, the Statement of Deposit, the ledger, the XX/XX/XXXX email documenting pre-existing issues, the XXXX response packet, and my dispute correspondence.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"FAIR COLLECTIONS & OUTSOURCING, INC.",TX,75019,,Web,2025-11-20,Closed with explanation,Yes
